{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"Before calling arbitrage “failed,” the board should establish whether the observed closing gap is real, persistent, and actionable:\n\n- **Check valuation timing and market hours.** Compare the ETF’s closing price with a contemporaneous, executable estimate of portfolio value—not a stale or indicative NAV. Review underlying holdings’ trading hours, closing-auction timing, fair-value adjustments, and any price or FX inputs that may be asynchronous.\n- **Compare peers and conditions.** Examine similar ETFs, spreads, depth, volatility, flows, and premium/discount behavior over the same dates. Determine whether the pattern is fund-specific or reflects broad market conditions.\n- **Investigate underlying frictions.** Review liquidity and spreads in the holdings, concentration, settlement, creation-unit composition, cash components, transaction costs, and any restrictions or operational delays that could make hedging or creation/redemption difficult.\n- **Assess intermediary participation.** Ask whether authorized participants (APs) and market makers remained active, quoted competitively, and could access both ETF and basket markets. Review their capacity, concentration, incentives, and any disruptions.\n\nRequest time-aligned data and explanations from the adviser, administrator, APs, and liquidity providers. Depending on findings, consider better valuation disclosures, basket or operational changes, broader AP engagement, or improved market-making support. Set monitoring and escalation criteria suited to the fund; do not presume one cause or impose a universal numerical trigger."}}
